<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 65 0 R/T(The Analysis :  For each customer I count the days since their latest order and group them as active \(30 days or less\), passive \(31 to 90\) or churned \(over 90\). Then I count customers per group by country and calculate the percentages.)/E(The Analysis :  For each customer I count the days since their latest order and group them as active \(30 days or less\), passive \(31 to 90\) or churned \(over 90\). Then I count customers per group by country and calculate the percentages.)/Pg 16 0 R/K[42]>>
endobj

291 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 65 0 R/T(Insight  Only 10% of customers are active \(ordered in the last 30 days\) and 10% are passive \(31 to 90 days\). The other 80% have not ordered in over 90 days, and this holds everywhere: churn is 79 to 81% in all 12 markets with 100+ customers. The 8,042 passive customers are the easiest to win back, mostly in China, the US and Brazil. A retention program should then cover all markets.)/E(Insight  Only 10% of customers are active \(ordered in the last 30 days\) and 10% are passive \(31 to 90 days\). The other 80% have not ordered in over 90 days, and this holds everywhere: churn is 79 to 81% in all 12 markets with 100+ customers. The 8,042 passive customers are the easiest to win back, mostly in China, the US and Brazil. A retention program should then cover all markets.)/Pg 16 0 R/K[43]>>
