600 0 obj
<</Title(Insight  Outerwear & Coats \($200K\) and Jeans \($193K\) led 2024 and made up about 54% of the top five\220s sales. Jeans almost caught Outerwear at a 33% lower price because it sold 44% more items. Swim sold a lot of items \(1,755\) but at the lowest price sell \($56\), while Suits sold the fewest \(816\) sell at $129. Suggestion :  For Q4,We could push the top two, bundle Swim to raise basket size, and find out why Suits sells so little.)/Dest[12 0 R/Fit]/Parent 513 0 R/Prev 660 0 R>>
endobj

601 0 obj
<</Title(3\) Premium or Overpriced?)/Dest[13 0 R/Fit]/Parent 7 0 R/Prev 513 0 R/First 661 0 R/Last 661 0 R/Count -1/Next 662 0 R>>
endobj

602 0 obj
<</Title(5\) Refunds and Returns: The Hidden Cost to Sales)/Dest[15 0 R/Fit]/Parent 7 0 R/Prev 662 0 R/First 663 0 R/Last 664 0 R/Count -6/Next 514 0 R>>
endobj

603 0 obj
<</Title(country)/Dest[16 0 R/Fit]/Parent 514 0 R/Next 665 0 R>>
endobj

604 0 obj
<</Title(Insight  Only 10% of customers are active \(ordered in the last 30 days\) and 10% are passive \(31 to 90 days\). The other 80% have not ordered in over 90 days, and this holds everywhere: churn is 79 to 81% in all 12 markets with 100+ customers. The 8,042 passive customers are the easiest to win back, mostly in China, the US and Brazil. A retention program should then cover all markets.)/Dest[16 0 R/Fit]/Parent 514 0 R/Prev 666 0 R>>
endobj

